  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMost of the area will remain dry the rest of today, but a shortwave \ntrough will quickly move in from the northwest and may bring a few \nshowers and storms to southwest NH and possibly farther east toward \nConcord, Manchester, and Nashua through early this evening. \nEnvironmental soundings across this corridor show a few hundred J/kg \nof instability and sufficient wind shear that a storm or two could \nbe strong with gusty winds thanks to high dewpoint depressions in \nthe low levels. Freezing levels remain around 9000 ft, so small hail \nwill also be possible. This threat should end by sunset.\n\nFor tonight through Saturday night, Canadian high pressure will \nbuild toward the region tonight and then become centered over New \nEngland through Saturday night. Plenty of subsidence and dry air \nwill provide mostly sunny and dry conditions with highs in the 70s \nto low 80s Saturday along with comfortable humidity. We'll have also \nhave a couple of seasonably cool nights tonight and Saturday night \nwith good radiational cooling. Lows in the 40s are likely in \nthe northern valleys and typically cooler spots across the \ninterior...with lower 50s elsewhere. The light winds and mostly \nclear skies will also promote patchy valley fog each of these \nnights, and it will probably be cool enough for steam fog over \nmany area lakes and ponds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSunday begins with high pressure overhead, quickly exiting east\ninto the open Atlantic. Surface return flow combined with a WSW\nlow level flow will begin to saturate the low levels while high\nclouds overrun from the west. Right now, mid level saturation \nisn't likely until the late afternoon or evening, and this keeps\nrain chances lower through the day until the evening and \novernight hours.\n\nForecast ensemble IVT plumes differ in magnitude and \ntrajectory, with a more northeastward segment from the ECMWF ENS\nthan the GEFS (a northward suppression, more progressive \nthrough the Mid Atlantic). In line with this vapor transport in \nthe ENS, EFI QPF fields have also jumped comparing guidance from\nWednesday evening to Thursday evening's runs indicating some \npotential for an above average precipitation event. Shift of \ntails however remains muted, with an extreme precipitation event\nunlikely. \n\nWarm cloud depth in deterministic models is impressive, with \nruns of the GFS, ECMWF, NAM, and Canadian all depicting depths \ngreater than 12kft for a period late Sunday night and early \nMonday morning. Coinciding with above normal PWATs around 2 \ninches, a period of heavy rain is becoming more likely, but \nexact location and radar presentation will need to be resolved \nwith time.\n\nOne of the larger differences in guidance, and therefore an\nimportant distinction between outcomes, is how far south the\ntrough or low cuts into southern New England. A track further\ninto southern New England could kink vapor transport, limiting\nmoisture depth and duration, while a flatter path across\nnorthern New England keeps this SW moisture flux open, resulting\nin more efficient rain processes.\n\nRemaining rain tapers off through the day Monday, with drier\nweather and a bit of a warmup through midweek as highs climb\ninto the lower to mid 80s. The next chance for rain then looks\nto be Wednesday into Thursday as an elongated cold front\napproaches from Quebec. It is yet to be determined if a wave\ndevelops out of this front, but could offer some rain\nenhancement depending on location through northern New England.",
